					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2203795&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607418073" alt="Kalinin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2203795)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;" >Kalinin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1443743014 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Oct 2015 23:43</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2384978">
									

<p>Couple of nitpicks to start:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These entities behave in a manner consistent with retail employees</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This has no real descriptive value.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Exposed persons react to anomalous properties of SCP-2523, including those of trades, entities, and objects originating from the anomaly as though entirely quotidian.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The &quot;entirely quotidian&quot; part seems way too subjective for the context here. Seems like there's a better way to describe the fact that weird shit happening is considered normal by the customers here.</p>
<p>Writing's all right other than those points. My issues:</p>
<ul>
<li>I'm not really into fantasy creatures and concepts actually existing as a starting point. That's not something that I would say is wrong or that people shouldn't do, but it is affecting the portion of my vote that's up to personal preference. I kind of feel like I've seen enough mischievious faeries<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-14791-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-14791-1')">1</a></sup> around here.</li>
<li>There are so many compulsion effects. People are compelled to not interfere with access to it. The things people buy compel them to do stuff. You're compelled to forget about them. No single instance of this device really sinks this, but taken all together, it runs into the same old issue where the thing does what it does because compulsion rather than any intrinsic qualities.</li>
<li>The fact that it can't be interfered with except through indirect means feels like a contrived means of horror.</li>
<li>The interview doesn't really work for me. I don't find that it adds much to the narrative, and all it really does when I read it is reinforce this &quot;tra la la oh you delightful human folk&quot; tone that once again sounds like a mischievous faerie from central casting.</li>
</ul>
<p>Not terrible, but not really moving the needle for me either.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-14791-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-14791-1')">1</a>. Yes, I'm aware they're supposed to be goblins, however they act exactly like the mischievious faeries that are increasingly in evidence on the site.</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
					
