<p>Well, I don't know if I can address your more systemic problems but I did address both of your nitpicks, and replaced the compulsions with more interesting effects.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The fact that it can't be interfered with except through indirect means feels like a contrived means of horror.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think I like writing about a Foundation that doesn't always get to have perfect containment, and that my view of the good folk is that they are very hard to keep imprisoned.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The interview doesn't really work for me. I don't find that it adds much to the narrative, and all it really does when I read it is reinforce this &quot;tra la la oh you delightful human folk&quot; tone that once again sounds like a mischievous faerie from central casting.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well, it is supposed to be <em>the</em> mischievous fairy from central casting. &quot;Bobby Goodman&quot; was deliberate. The interview was also supposed to convey some things about this version of the faerie otherworld (notably that time is a spatial dimension and that the goblins are a free people that live in the liminal zone between summer and winter). As for the rest, I can only apologize for not entertaining you as he would:</p>
